WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  nicholas sarkozy failed to be re-elected  as french president in 2012 despite a  lavish campaign the scale of his rallies  was designed to impress but instead it  set off alarm bells a police  investigation accused sarkozy and his  campaign team of spending more than  double the 27 million legal limit set by  the french state and hiring a pr company  called big malian to help cover up the  overspending with a system of fake  invoices symbolically it's important to  say that a president  doesn't get any immunity  and has to respond to the law  it is also important because the  campaign for 2022 is coming so we have  to have this reflection about  how to prevent illegal financing of a  political campaign in may sarkozy faced  trial in paris for a legal campaign  funding alongside 13 co-accused among  them one of his former campaign managers  jerome lavroller who admitted a role in  the system of fake invoices but said it  had not been his idea in his only court  appearance during the one month trial  sarkozy denied any wrongdoing he said he  wasn't involved in or aware of the  overspending cover-up but prosecutors  said that sarkozy failed to supervise  his campaign financing and ignored  warnings from his accountants  sarkozy served one term as president  from 2007  a straight talker with a love of luxury  he was tough on immigration and crime he  retired from politics in 2017. in recent  years the political right wing has  struggled in france but sarkozy's  remained prominent for a lot of french  citizens who  vote for the right side nicolas sarkozy  is seen as this kind of  all-time somehow reassuring figure if  sarkozy is found guilty in the big  malian trial he could face a six-month  jail term it would be another blow to  his reputation in march he was convicted  of corruption in a different case and he  could be back in court soon he's accused  of receiving illegal campaign funds from  the late libyan leader muhammad gaddafi  sarkozy's legal problems it seems are  far from over natasha butler al jazeera  paris

